1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,575
SELinux has hundreds of switches, called booleans.

2
00:00:03,595 --> 00:00:07,919
Need a web server to read home directories? You do not write policy.

3
00:00:07,939 --> 00:00:12,775
You flip one switch. Today we read booleans, and change them, the right way.

4
00:00:13,245 --> 00:00:18,046
In this video: use Boolean settings to modify system SELinux settings.

5
00:00:22,566 --> 00:00:25,149
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:25,619 --> 00:00:29,439
Use Boolean settings to modify system SELinux settings.

7
00:00:29,909 --> 00:00:33,928
We'll read a boolean, change it until the next reboot, change it for good,

8
00:00:33,948 --> 00:00:35,457
and check it after a reboot.

9
00:00:42,977 --> 00:00:46,812
A boolean is an on or off switch inside the SELinux policy.

10
00:00:46,832 --> 00:00:50,667
One boolean allows the web server to read home directories.

11
00:00:50,687 --> 00:00:53,215
Another lets it connect to the network.

12
00:00:53,235 --> 00:00:57,528
Each switch is a rule you can turn on, without writing any policy.

13
00:01:02,998 --> 00:01:06,135
Three commands. getsebool reads a boolean.

14
00:01:06,155 --> 00:01:10,120
setsebool changes it, but only until the next reboot.

15
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:14,405
And setsebool with dash capital P changes it permanently:

16
00:01:14,425 --> 00:01:17,036
it is saved, and survives a reboot.

17
00:01:24,556 --> 00:01:30,480
First, read. getsebool with a name prints one boolean: httpd enable homedirs is off.

18
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:32,957
With dash a, it prints all of them,

19
00:01:32,977 --> 00:01:37,556
so we filter with grep for the ones that start with httpd enable.

20
00:01:37,576 --> 00:01:43,288
Then semanage boolean with dash l adds a description, and two values in brackets:

21
00:01:43,308 --> 00:01:46,189
the current value, and the default value.

22
00:01:50,709 --> 00:01:55,444
Now change it, until the next reboot. setsebool, the name, and on.

23
00:01:55,464 --> 00:02:01,424
getsebool confirms: on. And in the list, the brackets say: current on, default off.

24
00:02:01,444 --> 00:02:03,873
The change is live, but not saved.

25
00:02:08,393 --> 00:02:13,459
Now make it permanent. The same command, with dash capital P, for permanent.

26
00:02:13,479 --> 00:02:18,611
It takes a few seconds, because it rebuilds the policy. Now the brackets say:

27
00:02:18,631 --> 00:02:23,831
on, on. And semanage with dash l and dash capital C lists only the booleans we

28
00:02:23,851 --> 00:02:25,504
changed from the default.

29
00:02:30,024 --> 00:02:34,825
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot when the task needs one, and your turn.

30
00:02:36,345 --> 00:02:41,271
We reboot, and getsebool says: on. The permanent change survived. Compare:

31
00:02:41,291 --> 00:02:44,346
without dash capital P, it would be off again.

32
00:02:48,866 --> 00:02:54,201
Back to the default. setsebool with dash capital P and off. getsebool says off.

33
00:02:54,221 --> 00:02:59,081
The list of local changes still shows the boolean, now with off and off:

34
00:02:59,101 --> 00:03:03,758
the same as the default. Always put back what you changed for a demo.

35
00:03:08,278 --> 00:03:13,136
Your turn. Turn on the boolean httpd can network connect, permanently.

36
00:03:13,156 --> 00:03:17,943
Run the checker, then reboot and run it again: both checks must pass.

37
00:03:28,413 --> 00:03:30,164
Try before you open the solution.

38
00:03:35,684 --> 00:03:37,305
That was the last objective.

39
00:03:37,325 --> 00:03:42,171
Go back through the playlist, redo every practice task, and good luck on your exam.

40
00:03:42,641 --> 00:03:44,839
Subscribe, and see you in the next series.
